  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1233 AM MDT Thu Oct 1 2026\n\nWelcome to the final quarter of the 2026. And if there is a rule of \nthumb for the past nine months, it is that has been a very warm year \non average. Most of our major climate sites have had either their \nwarmest or second warmest year on record so far this year. And most \nof the next several days will be more of the same.\n\nThere are some minor concerns to worry about. The main feature of \nthe day is a weak cold front that will drop southward into the \nnorthern Wyoming today. There is not a ton of moisture to work with \nand the best jet forcing should remain in Montana. There may be just \nenough moisture and forcing for a stray shower over the Bighorns or \nJohnson County, but the chance is generally 1 out of 6 or less, with \nmost locations remaining dry. There will also be a bit of a breeze \nin that location, but nothing out of the ordinary. Otherwise, it \nwill be another dry and nice autumn day, although somewhat cooler \nEast of the Divide. \n\nOn Friday the concern shifts to fire weather. Another weak cold \nfront will be approaching from the north. Flow will turn \nsouthwesterly ahead of the front, bringing downsloping flow and a \nwarm day, with some locations seeing high temperatures rising into \nthe 80s in the warmer spots. With the tightening pressure gradient, \na gusty breeze will develop in the favored southwesterly flow areas \nfrom eastern Sweetwater County to Natrona County. As for potential \nfire weather highlights, there will be enough wind. However, \nhumidity looks borderline at best. The NBM ensemble gives a mean \nminimum humidity generally only around 18 to 22 percent even in the \nfavored areas, and it may not occur for three hours. So, no fire \nweather highlights at this point. There will likely be elevated fire \nweather for a time in the afternoon in the favored locations. \nOtherwise, it will be another dry and warm day.\n\nSaturday will see temperatures all back a bit East of the Divide \nfollowing the frontal passage. Like today's front, it has little \nmoisture to work with so few or no showers are expected. There will \nbe a somewhat tighter pressure gradient though, especially across \nthe northwest flow / cold advection areas. The NBM ensemble gives at \nleast 1 out of 2 chance of wind gusts over 30 mph, with a 1 in 2 \nchance of gusts over 40 mph in northern Johnson County. Fire weather \nshould remain below critical though as cooler temperatures and \nhigher humidity move into the area. \n\nSunday looks like a dry and mild day with few concerns. Another weak \nsystem then approaches Monday into Monday night and could bring a \nfew showers. but again chances are small, generally less than 1 out \nof 4 in any locations and mainly in western Wyoming. There will \nlikely be more fire weather concerns at times next week, with Monday \nlooking like the day of the most concern with breezy conditions and \nlow humidity developing with the southwest flow ahead of the system. \nMuch of next week looks like a continuation of above average \ntemperatures and little chance of rain as ridging controls the \nweather across most of the western United States.",
